::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q01::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify x^5 * x^2. {
=x^7 #Correct. Same-base multiplication means add exponents: 5+2=7.
~x^10 #Not quite. This multiplies the exponents instead of adding them.
~2x^7 #Not quite. The bases are not added as coefficients.
~x^3 #Not quite. This subtracts exponents instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q02::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify a^9 / a^4. {
~a^13 #Not quite. This adds exponents, but division uses subtraction.
=a^5 #Correct. Same-base division means subtract exponents: 9-4=5.
~a^(9/4) #Not quite. The exponents are subtracted, not divided.
~1 #Not quite. The exponents are not equal.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q03::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (y^3)^5. {
~y^8 #Not quite. This adds exponents, but a power raised to a power uses multiplication.
~y^2 #Not quite. This subtracts exponents, but there is no division.
=y^15 #Correct. Multiply the exponents: 3*5=15.
~5y^3 #Not quite. The outside exponent is not a coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q04::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (4x)^2. {
~4x^2 #Not quite. The exponent must also apply to 4.
~8x^2 #Not quite. 4^2 is 16, not 8.
~16x #Not quite. The exponent must apply to x as well.
=16x^2 #Correct. Apply the exponent to each factor: 4^2=16 and x^2.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q05::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify b^6 * b^4. {
=b^10 #Correct. Same-base multiplication means add exponents: 6+4=10.
~b^24 #Not quite. This multiplies the exponents instead of adding them.
~2b^10 #Not quite. The variable base is not added to itself as a coefficient.
~b^2 #Not quite. This subtracts exponents instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q06::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify c^12 / c^5. {
~c^17 #Not quite. This adds exponents, but division uses subtraction.
=c^7 #Correct. Same-base division means subtract exponents: 12-5=7.
~c^(12/5) #Not quite. The exponents are subtracted, not divided.
~7c #Not quite. The result is a power of c, not a coefficient times c.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q07::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (n^4)^3. {
~n^7 #Not quite. This adds exponents, but a power raised to a power uses multiplication.
~n #Not quite. This subtracts exponents, but there is no division.
=n^12 #Correct. Multiply the exponents: 4*3=12.
~3n^4 #Not quite. The outside exponent is not a coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q08::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (3m)^3. {
~3m^3 #Not quite. The exponent must also apply to 3.
~9m^3 #Not quite. 3^3 is 27, not 9.
~27m #Not quite. The exponent must apply to m as well.
=27m^3 #Correct. Apply the exponent to each factor: 3^3=27 and m^3.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q09::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify 2x^3 * 5x^4. {
=10x^7 #Correct. Multiply coefficients 2*5=10 and add exponents 3+4=7.
~7x^7 #Not quite. The coefficients should be multiplied, not added.
~10x^12 #Not quite. The exponents should be added, not multiplied.
~10x #Not quite. The variable powers were not combined correctly.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q10::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify 18p^8 / 3p^2. {
~6p^10 #Not quite. The exponents should be subtracted for division.
=6p^6 #Correct. Divide coefficients 18/3=6 and subtract exponents 8-2=6.
~15p^6 #Not quite. The coefficients should be divided, not subtracted.
~6p^4 #Not quite. The exponent subtraction is 8-2=6, not 4.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q11::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (2r^5)^2. {
~2r^10 #Not quite. The outside exponent must also apply to 2.
~4r^7 #Not quite. The exponents should be multiplied, not added.
=4r^10 #Correct. 2^2=4 and (r^5)^2=r^10.
~4r^25 #Not quite. The exponents should be multiplied as 5*2, not 5*5.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q12::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (xy)^5. {
~xy^5 #Not quite. The exponent applies to both x and y.
~x^5y #Not quite. The exponent applies to both x and y.
~5xy #Not quite. The exponent is not a coefficient.
=x^5y^5 #Correct. The power of a product applies to each factor.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q13::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which property justifies d^4 * d^6 = d^10? {
=Product of powers #Correct. Same-base multiplication means add exponents.
~Quotient of powers #Not quite. Quotient of powers applies to division.
~Power of a power #Not quite. Power of a power applies when a power is raised to another power.
~Power of a product #Not quite. Power of a product applies to parentheses with an outside exponent.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q14::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which property justifies h^11 / h^3 = h^8? {
~Product of powers #Not quite. Product of powers applies to multiplication.
=Quotient of powers #Correct. Same-base division means subtract exponents.
~Power of a power #Not quite. Power of a power applies when a power is raised to another power.
~Power of a product #Not quite. Power of a product applies to factors inside parentheses.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q15::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which property justifies (k^2)^7 = k^14? {
~Product of powers #Not quite. Product of powers applies to multiplication of same-base powers.
~Quotient of powers #Not quite. Quotient of powers applies to division of same-base powers.
=Power of a power #Correct. A power raised to another power means multiply exponents.
~Power of a quotient #Not quite. There is no quotient in the expression.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q16::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which expression is equivalent to (5z)^2? {
~5z^2 #Not quite. The exponent must apply to 5 as well.
~10z^2 #Not quite. 5^2 is 25, not 10.
~25z #Not quite. The exponent must apply to z as well.
=25z^2 #Correct. Apply the exponent to each factor: 5^2=25 and z^2.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q17::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify q^3 * q^2 * q^4. {
=q^9 #Correct. Add all exponents with the same base: 3+2+4=9.
~q^24 #Not quite. This multiplies the exponents instead of adding them.
~3q^9 #Not quite. The repeated variable factors do not create a coefficient of 3.
~q^5 #Not quite. One exponent was not included.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q18::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify s^15 / s^9. {
~s^24 #Not quite. This adds exponents, but division uses subtraction.
=s^6 #Correct. Subtract exponents: 15-9=6.
~s^(15/9) #Not quite. The exponents are subtracted, not divided.
~6s #Not quite. The result is s^6, not 6s.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q19::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (v^6)^2. {
~v^8 #Not quite. This adds exponents instead of multiplying them.
~v^4 #Not quite. This subtracts exponents, but there is no division.
=v^12 #Correct. Multiply the exponents: 6*2=12.
~2v^6 #Not quite. The outside exponent is not a coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q20::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (6w^2)^2. {
~6w^4 #Not quite. The outside exponent must also apply to 6.
~12w^4 #Not quite. 6^2 is 36, not 12.
~36w^2 #Not quite. The exponent must apply to w^2 as well.
=36w^4 #Correct. 6^2=36 and (w^2)^2=w^4.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q21::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which expression is equivalent to x^(3/2)? {
=x*sqrt(x) #Correct. x^(3/2)=x^1*x^(1/2), and x^(1/2)=sqrt(x).
~sqrt(x^2) #Not quite. This is equivalent to x for nonnegative x, not x^(3/2).
~x^3/2 #Not quite. The exponent 3/2 does not mean divide x^3 by 2.
~x + sqrt(x) #Not quite. The factors multiply; they are not added.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q22::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which expression is equivalent to 6x^(3/2)? {
~6sqrt(x) #Not quite. This keeps only the x^(1/2) part and loses the x^1 factor.
=6x*sqrt(x) #Correct. x^(3/2)=x^1*x^(1/2), so 6x^(3/2)=6x*sqrt(x).
~6x^3/2 #Not quite. The exponent 3/2 does not mean divide 6x^3 by 2.
~6x + sqrt(x) #Not quite. The equivalent factors multiply; they are not added.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q23::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which expression is equivalent to z^(5/2)? {
~z^5/2 #Not quite. The exponent 5/2 does not mean divide z^5 by 2.
~sqrt(z^2) #Not quite. This represents z for nonnegative z, not z^(5/2).
=z^2*sqrt(z) #Correct. z^(5/2)=z^2*z^(1/2), and z^(1/2)=sqrt(z).
~z^2 + sqrt(z) #Not quite. The equivalent factors multiply; they are not added.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q24::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which expression is equivalent to 4y^(7/2)? {
~4y^7/2 #Not quite. The exponent 7/2 does not mean divide 4y^7 by 2.
~4sqrt(y^3) #Not quite. This does not represent y^(7/2).
~4y^3 + sqrt(y) #Not quite. The equivalent factors multiply; they are not added.
=4y^3*sqrt(y) #Correct. y^(7/2)=y^3*y^(1/2), and y^(1/2)=sqrt(y).
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q25::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] A student simplifies x^4 * y^2 as xy^6. What is the best correction? {
=The bases are different, so the expression should remain x^4y^2. #Correct. Exponent laws combine exponents only for like bases.
~Add the exponents and keep xy^6 because multiplication is shown. #Not quite. Exponents combine only when the bases match.
~Multiply the exponents to get xy^8. #Not quite. The bases are different, so the exponents cannot be combined.
~Divide the exponents to get xy^2. #Not quite. Division of exponents is not the rule here.
}
